const AP_NOTE = 'The NIOS book covers Andhra only briefly; this section adds standard AP sources (P. Raghunatha Rao and others). ';

X('ap-geo',{lesson:'NIOS Lessons 1–2 (sources, geography); AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS’s general lessons apply directly to Andhra: geography shapes history, and historians must cross-check literary sources, inscriptions, coins and archaeology. Andhra’s geography has three parts: the fertile Krishna–Godavari delta and coastal plains, the Eastern Ghats, and the dry Rayalaseema plateau. The delta supported farming, Buddhism and sea trade; Rayalaseema’s hills gave forts and border kingdoms. Sources for early Andhra include the Aitareya Brahmana, Megasthenes, Ashokan edicts at Erragudi and Rajula Mandagiri, Bhattiprolu’s casket inscriptions, the Kalamalla inscription (earliest Telugu), Satavahana coins and the stupas of Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda.',
revise:['Krishna–Godavari delta → rice surplus → dense population → kingdoms and Buddhist centres.','Long coast + ports (Ghantasala, Motupalli, Masulipatnam) → trade with Rome and South-East Asia.','Rayalaseema plateau → dry, hilly → forts and frontier wars (Vijayanagara, Bahmani).','Literary sources (Aitareya, Megasthenes) + inscriptions (Erragudi, Bhattiprolu, Kalamalla) + coins + stupas → reconstruct early history.','Telangana plateau → tank irrigation → Kakatiya kingdom.'],
analysis:['Andhra’s regions developed differently: coast (trade, Buddhism), Telangana (tank irrigation, Kakatiyas), Rayalaseema (frontier, forts); this explains later regional movements.','Early Andhra history depends heavily on inscriptions and archaeology, so dates are often debated.'],
mind:['Andhra: land and sources',[['Regions',['Coastal Andhra','Rayalaseema','Telangana (till 2014)']],['Rivers',['Godavari','Krishna','Pennar','Tungabhadra']],['Literary',['Aitareya Brahmana','Megasthenes','Puranas']],['Inscriptions',['Erragudi','Bhattiprolu','Kalamalla']],['Archaeology',['Amaravati','Nagarjunakonda','Coins']]]],
table:['Sources for early Andhra',['Source','Type','What it tells'],[['Aitareya Brahmana','Literary','First mention of Andhras'],['Megasthenes’ Indica','Foreign account','Andhras militarily strong, walled towns'],['Erragudi, Rajula Mandagiri','Ashokan edicts','Andhra in Mauryan empire'],['Bhattiprolu','Casket inscriptions','Early Brahmi variant; Buddhism'],['Kalamalla','Inscription','Earliest Telugu (6th century)'],['Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda','Monuments','Buddhist art, royal patronage']]],
places:[['Erragudi',77.5,15.4],['Bhattiprolu',80.78,16.1],['Kalamalla',78.53,14.63],['Amaravati',80.36,16.57],['Nagarjunakonda',79.31,16.52],['Godavari delta',81.9,16.7],['Rayalaseema (Kurnool)',78.04,15.83]],
figs:['map_7.2'],
qa:[['How has geography influenced the history of Andhra Pradesh?',150,'Andhra’s geography shaped its history in clear ways. The fertile Krishna and Godavari deltas produced rice surpluses that supported dense populations, kingdoms such as the Satavahanas, Ikshvakus and Eastern Chalukyas, and wealthy Buddhist centres like Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda. The long coastline with ports such as Ghantasala, Motupalli and Masulipatnam linked Andhra to Rome, South-East Asia and later European traders. The Eastern Ghats separated the coast from the plateau. In Telangana, tank irrigation supported the Kakatiyas. Rayalaseema’s dry, rocky uplands, with forts such as Gandikota and Penukonda, became a frontier zone in wars between Vijayanagara and the Deccan Sultanates. These regional differences also explain later regional identities and movements. Rivers also served as routes for trade and the spread of religions such as Buddhism, which flourished along the Krishna valley. Thus geography explains both Andhra’s prosperity and its regional diversity.'],
['Discuss the sources for reconstructing the early history of Andhra.',250,'Reconstructing Andhra’s early history requires combining several kinds of sources, as NIOS advises for Indian history generally.\n\nLiterary sources come first. The Aitareya Brahmana is the earliest text to mention the Andhras, describing them as a people on the edge of Aryan society. The Puranas list Andhra kings, especially the Satavahanas. Megasthenes, the Greek envoy to Chandragupta Maurya, wrote that the Andhras had many walled towns and a large army. Buddhist texts mention Andhra regions, and Hsuan Tsang later visited Dhanyakataka.\n\nInscriptions are the most reliable sources. Ashoka’s edicts at Erragudi and Rajula Mandagiri in Kurnool district show that Andhra was part of the Mauryan empire. The Bhattiprolu casket inscriptions are early records in a variant of Brahmi. Satavahana inscriptions, such as the Nasik inscription of Gautami Balasri, describe kings, and Ikshvaku records at Nagarjunakonda show royal patronage. The Kalamalla inscription (6th century) is the earliest known inscription fully in Telugu.\n\nCoins help with chronology and economy: Satavahana lead, potin and ship coins show trade, and Roman coins found in Andhra prove links with Rome.\n\nArchaeology fills many gaps. Excavations at Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda, Dhulikatta, Kotilingala and Satanikota reveal stupas, towns, crafts and burials; prehistoric sites in Kurnool show the earliest settlements.\n\nEach source has limits: Puranas give conflicting king lists, inscriptions are often brief, and dates are debated. Only by cross-checking them can historians build a reliable picture of early Andhra.']]});

X('ap-pre',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 2 (prehistoric cultures, Kurnool and Nagarjunakonda sites)',
brief:'NIOS’s prehistoric maps mark Andhra sites, including Kurnool, Renigunta and Nagarjunakonda, among Palaeolithic and Neolithic settlements. Andhra shows all stages: Palaeolithic tools in the Kurnool caves and river valleys, Mesolithic microliths, Neolithic farming in the Krishna–Tungabhadra region and Megalithic burials with iron tools widely across the state.',
revise:['River valleys and caves (Kurnool) → Palaeolithic hunters.','Warmer climate → Mesolithic microliths → wider spread of settlements.','Neolithic farming and cattle-keeping → villages in the Krishna–Tungabhadra region.','Iron and Megalithic burials → organised chiefdoms → base for Satavahana-era society.'],
analysis:['Andhra’s prehistoric record is rich but less studied; Nagarjunakonda’s sequence was partly lost under the reservoir.','Megalithic sites in Andhra link prehistory with the early historic period, showing continuity of settlement.'],
mind:['Prehistoric Andhra',[['Palaeolithic',['Kurnool caves','Renigunta']],['Mesolithic',['Microliths']],['Neolithic',['Nagarjunakonda','Utnur region']],['Megalithic',['Stone circles','Iron tools']]]],
table:['Prehistoric Andhra',['Age','Sites','Features'],[['Palaeolithic','Kurnool caves (Betamcherla, Billa Surgam), Renigunta','Stone tools, cave shelters'],['Mesolithic','Many river sites','Microliths'],['Neolithic','Nagarjunakonda and Krishna–Tungabhadra sites','Farming, cattle'],['Megalithic','Across AP','Stone circles, iron tools']]],
places:[['Betamcherla caves',78.16,15.47],['Renigunta',79.51,13.65],['Nagarjunakonda',79.31,16.52],['Kurnool',78.04,15.83]],
figs:['map_2.3','map_2.4'],
qa:[['Describe the prehistoric cultures of Andhra Pradesh.',150,'Andhra Pradesh has evidence of all the major prehistoric stages. In the Palaeolithic age, hunters used hand axes and cleavers; their tools are found in the Kurnool caves, such as Betamcherla and Billa Surgam, at Renigunta and along river valleys. NIOS’s maps mark Kurnool, Renigunta and Nagarjunakonda among prehistoric sites. In the Mesolithic age small stone tools called microliths were used across many river sites. The Neolithic age brought polished stone axes, farming and cattle-keeping in the Krishna–Tungabhadra region, including the Nagarjunakonda valley. Finally, the Megalithic age left stone circles and other graves with iron tools and Black-and-Red Ware across the state. These cultures laid the foundation for early historic Andhra under the Satavahanas. These sites show continuous human presence in Andhra and the gradual move from hunting to farming and iron use, which prepared the ground for later states.'],
['Show how prehistoric Andhra prepared the ground for its early historic kingdoms.',250,'The early historic kingdoms of Andhra, beginning with the Satavahanas, did not appear from nothing; they built on a long prehistoric development.\n\nPalaeolithic hunter-gatherers lived in caves and river valleys, especially in Kurnool, using stone tools. They show that humans were present in Andhra for hundreds of thousands of years and adapted to its varied landscapes.\n\nIn the Mesolithic period, microliths allowed more efficient hunting and gathering, and people spread more widely along rivers, increasing contact between groups.\n\nThe Neolithic period brought the crucial step of food production. In the Krishna–Tungabhadra region, communities grew crops and kept cattle, using polished stone axes. Settled villages, pottery and storage appeared, creating surplus and larger communities.\n\nThe Megalithic period introduced iron, used for axes, sickles and weapons, which allowed forest clearance and expansion of farming, especially in the fertile deltas. Megalithic burials, found widely across Andhra, show organised communities, belief in an afterlife and chiefs able to mobilise labour, an early form of social hierarchy.\n\nThese developments created the conditions for the early historic period: agricultural surplus, iron technology, organised communities and chiefs. With the arrival of the Mauryan empire, whose edicts appear at Erragudi, and the spread of Buddhism and trade, these communities developed into towns and states. The Satavahanas then built a kingdom on this base, with towns like Dhanyakataka and Buddhist centres such as Amaravati. Thus prehistoric Andhra provided the economic and social foundations for its later kingdoms.']]});

X('ap-sata',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 6: Post-Mauryan Developments (Satavahanas, Amaravati school, trade)',
brief:'NIOS describes the Satavahanas as having established a kingdom in the Deccan between the Krishna and Godavari with their capital at Pratishthana (near Aurangabad), divided into districts called aharas or rashtras. The Dakshinapatha trade route linked north India to Pratishthana. The Amaravati school of art flourished under them in the lower Krishna–Godavari valleys (about 150 BC–AD 350). NIOS says the Satavahana kingdom was wiped out in the first quarter of the 3rd century AD and succeeded by the Ikshvakus. Its map shows the Satavahana and Kushana empires and Deccan sites such as Dharanikota and Amaravati.',
revise:['Mauryan decline → local chiefs in the Deccan → Satavahana kingdom (Simuka).','Gautamiputra Satakarni defeats the Shakas (Nahapana) → revival and expansion.','Dakshinapatha + sea ports → inland and Roman trade → wealth.','Wealth → land grants to Buddhist monks and brahmanas → Amaravati stupa and art school.','Weak later rulers + feudatories → decline early 3rd century → Ikshvakus in the Krishna valley.'],
analysis:['Satavahana origin is debated: Andhra (Puranas call them Andhras) or Maharashtra (early inscriptions near Nasik, Naneghat). A balanced answer mentions both.','They were the first to give land grants with tax exemptions to brahmanas and Buddhists, a practice that later grew into feudal tendencies.','Metronymics (Gautamiputra, Vasishthiputra) show the prestige of mothers, but society was still patriarchal.'],
mind:['Satavahanas',[['Rulers',['Simuka','Satakarni I','Hala','Gautamiputra','Yajna Sri']],['Administration',['Aharas','Amatyas','Land grants']],['Economy',['Dakshinapatha','Roman trade','Ship coins']],['Culture',['Amaravati stupa','Gathasaptasati','Prakrit']],['Decline',['Early 3rd century','Ikshvakus']]]],
table:['Satavahana rulers',['Ruler','Remember'],[['Simuka','Founder'],['Satakarni I','Ashvamedha; Naneghat inscription of Naganika'],['Hala','Gathasaptasati in Prakrit'],['Gautamiputra Satakarni','Greatest; defeated Nahapana; Nasik inscription of Gautami Balasri'],['Vasishthiputra Pulumavi','Amaravati expanded'],['Yajna Sri Satakarni','Last great ruler; ship coins']]],
places:[['Pratishthana (Paithan)',75.38,19.48],['Dhanyakataka (Dharanikota)',80.33,16.58],['Amaravati',80.36,16.57],['Nasik',73.79,19.99],['Naneghat',73.68,19.29],['Kotilingala',78.95,18.85],['Bhattiprolu',80.78,16.1]],
figs:['map_6.2','map_6.3'],
qa:[['Describe the administration and economy of the Satavahanas.',150,'The Satavahana kingdom, centred on the Deccan with its capital at Pratishthana, had a simple but effective administration. The king was assisted by officials called amatyas and mahamatras. NIOS notes that the kingdom was divided into districts called aharas or rashtras, each under an amatya; villages were the basic units. Military chiefs held local power in some areas. The Satavahanas were among the first to grant land with tax exemptions to brahmanas and Buddhist monks. The economy prospered through agriculture in the fertile Krishna–Godavari region, crafts organised in guilds, and trade. The Dakshinapatha route linked the Deccan with north India, and ports traded with Rome. Their lead, potin and ship-type coins show active commerce and maritime trade. The Satavahanas also patronised Buddhism generously, and their prosperity supported great centres like Amaravati, showing a close link between trade, wealth and religion.'],
['Assess the contribution of the Satavahanas to the history and culture of Andhra.',250,'The Satavahanas, who ruled the Deccan from about the 1st century BC to the early 3rd century AD, were the first great dynasty of Andhra history, and their contribution was lasting.\n\nPolitically, they created the first large state in the Deccan after the Mauryas. Simuka founded the dynasty; Gautamiputra Satakarni revived it by defeating the Shaka ruler Nahapana, as the Nasik inscription of his mother Gautami Balasri records; Yajna Sri Satakarni was the last great ruler. They organised the kingdom into aharas under amatyas, as NIOS notes.\n\nEconomically, they made the Deccan a hub of trade. The Dakshinapatha linked their capital Pratishthana with north India, and ports on both coasts traded with Rome. Guilds of craftsmen and merchants flourished, and their coins of lead and potin, some showing ships, reflect maritime commerce.\n\nSocially and religiously, they followed Vedic religion, performing sacrifices, but they generously supported Buddhism. They began the practice of land grants to brahmanas and monks, which spread agriculture and religion. Their use of metronymics shows the prestige of royal mothers.\n\nCulturally, their greatest legacy is in art and literature. The Amaravati stupa and the Amaravati school of art, which NIOS describes as flourishing in the lower Krishna–Godavari valleys, produced exquisite narrative sculpture that influenced Sri Lanka and South-East Asia. King Hala compiled the Gathasaptasati in Prakrit, a classic of love poetry. They also promoted Prakrit as an administrative language.\n\nThus the Satavahanas laid the political, economic and cultural foundations on which later Andhra dynasties, beginning with the Ikshvakus, built.']]});

X('ap-ikshvaku',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 6 (Ikshvakus succeed Satavahanas; Amaravati school) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS notes that the Ikshvakus succeeded the Satavahanas in the early 3rd century AD, and its Deccan map marks Nagarjunakonda. The Ikshvakus ruled the Krishna valley from Vijayapuri (Nagarjunakonda). Kings performed Vedic sacrifices, while royal women generously supported Buddhism. Nagarjunakonda became a great centre of stupas, monasteries and sculpture in the Amaravati style, and is linked by tradition with the philosopher Acharya Nagarjuna.',
revise:['Satavahana decline → Ikshvakus in the Krishna valley (early 3rd century).','King Santamula’s Vedic sacrifices (Ashvamedha) → claims to sovereignty.','Royal women’s patronage (Chantisri) → stupas and viharas at Nagarjunakonda.','Trade and agrarian wealth → Buddhist art in Amaravati style → Nagarjunakonda as international centre.','Decline after Rudrapurushadatta → smaller dynasties (Brihatphalayanas, Salankayanas).'],
analysis:['The Ikshvaku court shows religious coexistence: Vedic rituals by kings and Buddhist patronage by queens.','Nagarjunakonda’s excavation and relocation before the dam is an important example of salvage archaeology.'],
mind:['Ikshvakus',[['Rulers',['Santamula','Virapurushadatta','Ehuvala','Rudrapurushadatta']],['Capital',['Vijayapuri']],['Religion',['Vedic kings','Buddhist queens']],['Art',['Nagarjunakonda stupas','Amaravati style']]]],
table:['Ikshvaku rulers',['Ruler','Remember'],[['Vasishthiputra Santamula','Founder; Ashvamedha'],['Virapurushadatta','Buddhism at its height'],['Ehuvala Chantamula','Temples also built'],['Rudrapurushadatta','Last known king']]],
places:[['Vijayapuri (Nagarjunakonda)',79.31,16.52],['Jaggayyapeta',80.1,16.89],['Amaravati',80.36,16.57]],
figs:['map_7.2'],
qa:[['Write a note on the Ikshvakus of Vijayapuri.',150,'The Ikshvakus ruled the lower Krishna valley in the 3rd and 4th centuries AD after the decline of the Satavahanas, with their capital at Vijayapuri, the site of Nagarjunakonda. Their founder Vasishthiputra Santamula performed Vedic sacrifices such as the Ashvamedha to proclaim his sovereignty. Under Virapurushadatta, Buddhism flourished as never before. Royal women, notably Chantisri, made large gifts for stupas, monasteries and chaityas. Nagarjunakonda became a major Buddhist centre with monks from Sri Lanka and elsewhere, and its sculptures continued the Amaravati tradition. Later rulers like Ehuvala Chantamula also built Hindu temples. The dynasty declined after Rudrapurushadatta. Its monuments were excavated and partly moved before the Nagarjuna Sagar dam flooded the valley. Inscriptions at Nagarjunakonda record these gifts in detail, making them a valuable source for the social and religious history of the period.'],
['Explain the role of the Ikshvakus in the history of Buddhism in Andhra.',250,'The Ikshvakus of Vijayapuri (3rd–4th century AD) presided over one of the most important phases of Buddhism in Andhra.\n\nAlthough the kings themselves, beginning with Santamula, followed Vedic religion and performed sacrifices such as the Ashvamedha, their court was marked by religious tolerance. Royal women, especially Chantisri, the sister of Santamula, and other queens and princesses, gave generous donations to Buddhist institutions. Inscriptions at Nagarjunakonda record these gifts for the Mahachaitya and many viharas.\n\nUnder Virapurushadatta, Buddhism reached its height. Nagarjunakonda grew into a large Buddhist complex with stupas, chaityas, monasteries and a university-like centre. Monks of different schools, including the Mahasanghikas and Theravadins, lived there, and there were links with Sri Lanka and other Buddhist lands. Tradition associates the area with Acharya Nagarjuna, the great Mahayana philosopher of the Madhyamaka school, which is how the site got its name.\n\nArtistically, the Ikshvaku period continued and refined the Amaravati school. Limestone panels depict Jataka stories and scenes from the Buddha’s life with graceful, lively figures.\n\nEconomically, trade along the Krishna and to the coast, and agricultural prosperity, supported these institutions; merchants and guilds also made donations.\n\nThe Ikshvaku period thus shows Buddhism at its peak in Andhra, flourishing alongside Vedic religion. After the dynasty declined, Buddhism slowly weakened as Brahmanical religion grew under later dynasties. The excavation of Nagarjunakonda before the dam was built preserved much of this heritage, now displayed in the island museum.']]});

X('ap-postik',{lesson:'AP sources (not covered in detail by NIOS); NIOS Lesson 7 (Deccan kingdoms context)',
brief:AP_NOTE+'Between the Ikshvakus and the Eastern Chalukyas (c. 4th–7th century), several smaller dynasties ruled parts of Andhra: the Brihatphalayanas (Jayavarma), the Anandagotrikas of the Guntur area, the Salankayanas of Vengi (near Eluru), and finally the Vishnukundins, who united much of coastal Andhra and Telangana. Madhava Varma II was the greatest Vishnukundin ruler; they supported Vedic religion and are linked with the Undavalli caves. Pulakesin II of Badami conquered Vengi, and in 624 his brother founded the Eastern Chalukya dynasty. Meanwhile, the Renati Cholas ruled Rayalaseema and issued the earliest Telugu inscription at Kalamalla.',
revise:['Ikshvaku decline → small regional dynasties (Brihatphalayana, Anandagotrika, Salankayana).','Vengi as a centre → fertile delta → contested capital.','Vishnukundins unite coast and Telangana → Vedic revival, cave temples (Undavalli).','Badami Chalukya expansion under Pulakesin II → conquest of Vengi → Eastern Chalukyas (624).','Renati Cholas in Rayalaseema → Kalamalla inscription → beginnings of written Telugu.'],
analysis:['This period saw a shift from Buddhism towards Vedic and Puranic religion in Andhra.','The order of some Vishnukundin kings is debated; mention this caution in answers.'],
mind:['After the Ikshvakus',[['Small dynasties',['Brihatphalayanas','Anandagotrikas','Salankayanas']],['Vishnukundins',['Indra Varma','Madhava Varma II','Undavalli']],['Rayalaseema',['Renati Cholas','Kalamalla']],['End',['Pulakesin II','Vengi 624']]]],
table:['Dynasties c. 300–624',['Dynasty','Centre','Remember'],[['Brihatphalayanas','Kodur','Jayavarma'],['Anandagotrikas','Guntur area','Kandara'],['Salankayanas','Vengi (Pedavegi)','Vedic religion'],['Vishnukundins','Indrapalanagara and coast','Madhava Varma II; Undavalli'],['Renati Cholas','Rayalaseema','Kalamalla inscription']]],
places:[['Vengi (Pedavegi)',81.12,16.79],['Indrapalanagara (Nalgonda)',79.27,17.05],['Undavalli',80.58,16.5],['Kalamalla',78.53,14.63],['Guntur',80.44,16.3]],
figs:[],
qa:[['Write a note on the Vishnukundins.',150,'The Vishnukundins ruled parts of Andhra and Telangana from about the 5th century until 624 AD, filling the gap between the Ikshvakus and the Eastern Chalukyas. Their founder is generally given as Indra Varma, and their early capital was Indrapalanagara in the Nalgonda region, later shifting towards the coast. Their greatest ruler, Madhava Varma II, extended the kingdom widely and performed many Vedic sacrifices, including the Ashvamedha. The Vishnukundins supported Vedic and Puranic religion, though some also helped Buddhist monasteries. The rock-cut Undavalli caves near Vijayawada are linked with them. Their rule ended when Pulakesin II of Badami conquered Vengi, and his brother Kubja Vishnuvardhana founded the Eastern Chalukya dynasty in 624. The order of their middle kings is still debated. Their period marks a shift in Andhra from Buddhist dominance towards Vedic and Puranic religion, which continued under later dynasties.'],
['Discuss the political and cultural developments in Andhra between the Ikshvakus and the Eastern Chalukyas.',250,'The period from the 4th to the early 7th century in Andhra was one of political fragmentation followed by consolidation, with important religious changes.\n\nAfter the Ikshvakus declined, several smaller dynasties ruled parts of the region. The Brihatphalayanas, known from King Jayavarma’s Kodur grant, ruled a small area. The Anandagotrikas held the Guntur region. The Salankayanas ruled from Vengi, near Eluru, which became an important political centre in the fertile delta. In the south, Pallava influence reached southern Andhra.\n\nThe Vishnukundins then united much of coastal Andhra and Telangana. Madhava Varma II, their greatest king, conquered widely and performed Vedic sacrifices. Their rule provided a larger political framework that the Eastern Chalukyas later inherited.\n\nCulturally, the period marks a shift from the dominance of Buddhism, seen under the Satavahanas and Ikshvakus, towards Vedic and Puranic Hinduism. Kings performed Vedic rituals and made land grants to brahmanas, which spread agriculture and Brahmanical culture. Rock-cut cave temples such as those at Undavalli reflect this change. Buddhism continued but with declining royal support.\n\nIn Rayalaseema, the Renati Cholas ruled and issued the Kalamalla inscription in the 6th century, the earliest known inscription fully in Telugu, marking the emergence of Telugu as a written language.\n\nThe period ended with the expansion of the Badami Chalukyas. Pulakesin II conquered Vengi, and in 624 his brother Kubja Vishnuvardhana founded the Eastern Chalukya dynasty, beginning a long and stable rule over coastal Andhra.']]});

X('ap-chalukya',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 7 (Pulakesin II, Chalukyas) and Lesson 8 (Telugu literature: Tikkana completed the Mahabharata); AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS describes Pulakesin II (610–642) as the greatest Badami Chalukya ruler; his conquests in the east led to the founding of the Eastern Chalukya line at Vengi in 624 by his brother Kubja Vishnuvardhana. In its early medieval lesson NIOS notes the growth of regional languages and that the Telugu Mahabharata, begun earlier, was completed by Tikkana in the 13th century. The Eastern Chalukyas ruled coastal Andhra for about 450 years, fought the Rashtrakutas, allied by marriage with the Cholas, and under Rajaraja Narendra (1019–1061) patronised Nannaya, who began the Telugu Mahabharata. Kulottunga I merged Vengi with the Chola empire in 1070.',
revise:['Pulakesin II’s conquest of Vengi → Kubja Vishnuvardhana founds Eastern Chalukyas (624).','Rich delta + strategic position → wars with Rashtrakutas (north-west) → Gunaga Vijayaditya III’s victories.','Rashtrakuta pressure → marriage alliance with Cholas → Chola influence in Vengi.','Stable rule + royal patronage → Telugu literature begins (Nannaya under Rajaraja Narendra).','Chola–Chalukya marriages → Kulottunga I unites Vengi with the Chola empire (1070).'],
analysis:['The Eastern Chalukyas made Telugu a literary language; this is their most lasting legacy.','Vengi was caught between larger powers (Rashtrakutas, Cholas, Western Chalukyas of Kalyani), which explains its alliances and wars.','Temples like Draksharama and Biccavolu show the growth of Shaiva religion and a regional style.'],
mind:['Eastern Chalukyas',[['Founding',['Kubja Vishnuvardhana','624']],['Wars',['Rashtrakutas','Gunaga Vijayaditya III']],['Chola link',['Marriages','Rajaraja Narendra','Kulottunga I 1070']],['Culture',['Nannaya','Draksharama','Biccavolu']],['Capitals',['Vengi','Rajahmundry']]]],
table:['Key Eastern Chalukya rulers',['Ruler','Remember'],[['Kubja Vishnuvardhana (624–641)','Founder'],['Vijayaditya II','Wars with Rashtrakutas; temples'],['Gunaga Vijayaditya III (9th century)','Great warrior'],['Chalukya Bhima I','Draksharama, Samarlakota temples'],['Rajaraja Narendra (1019–1061)','Nannaya; capital Rajahmundry'],['Kulottunga I (1070)','Merged Vengi with Cholas']]],
places:[['Vengi',81.12,16.79],['Rajahmundry',81.78,17.0],['Draksharama',82.06,16.79],['Biccavolu',82.05,16.95],['Samarlakota',82.18,17.05],['Badami',75.68,15.92],['Thanjavur (Cholas)',79.13,10.79]],
figs:['map_8.1'],
qa:[['Assess the contribution of Rajaraja Narendra to Telugu culture.',150,'Rajaraja Narendra (1019–1061) was one of the most important Eastern Chalukya kings. He moved the capital to Rajamahendravaram (Rajahmundry) on the Godavari. Closely linked to the Cholas by marriage, he faced rivalry from the Western Chalukyas of Kalyani and from his half-brother over the throne, but secured his rule with Chola support. His greatest contribution was to literature. He encouraged his court poet Nannaya to translate the Mahabharata into Telugu. Nannaya completed the Adi, Sabha and part of the Aranya parvas and shaped Telugu grammar and poetic style, earning the title Adikavi. Later Tikkana and Errana completed the epic. Thus Rajaraja Narendra’s patronage turned Telugu into a literary language, which is why his reign is remembered as a turning point. His reign thus marks the beginning of a long literary tradition, and Rajahmundry remains a symbol of Telugu literary heritage.'],
['Describe the political history and cultural achievements of the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi.',250,'The Eastern Chalukyas ruled coastal Andhra for about 450 years, from 624 to around 1070.\n\nTheir political history began with the conquest of Vengi by Pulakesin II of Badami, whom NIOS calls the greatest Chalukya ruler. In 624 his brother Kubja Vishnuvardhana became ruler of Vengi and founded an independent line. Vengi’s rich delta attracted powerful neighbours. The Eastern Chalukyas fought long wars with the Rashtrakutas; Vijayaditya II and especially Gunaga Vijayaditya III won notable victories. In the late 10th century the dynasty was disrupted by Telugu Choda rule, but restored with the help of the Cholas. Marriage alliances with the Cholas followed, and under Rajaraja Narendra (1019–1061) Chola influence was strong. Finally, Kulottunga I, a Chalukya prince with a Chola mother, became Chola emperor in 1070 and merged Vengi with the Chola empire.\n\nTheir cultural achievements were significant. They supported Shaiva religion, building temples at Draksharama, Samarlakota and Biccavolu that show an emerging Andhra style. Land grants to brahmanas spread agriculture and learning. Jainism also received some support.\n\nTheir most lasting achievement was in language and literature. Telugu inscriptions became more common, and under Rajaraja Narendra, Nannaya began the Telugu Mahabharata, establishing Telugu as a literary language. NIOS notes that regional languages grew in this period and that Tikkana later completed the Telugu Mahabharata.\n\nThus the Eastern Chalukyas gave coastal Andhra centuries of stable rule, linked it with the larger politics of south India, and laid the foundation of Telugu literary culture.']]});

X('ap-kakatiya',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 9 (Alauddin’s and Tughlaq campaigns against Warangal) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS mentions the Kakatiyas through the Sultanate campaigns: Malik Kafur’s expedition against Warangal, whose ruler Rai Rudra Dev (Prataparudra II) agreed to part with treasure and pay tribute, and the later attack by Ulugh Khan; it also notes Bhanudeva II of Odisha had helped Warangal. The Kakatiyas rose as feudatories of the Kalyani Chalukyas, became independent under Rudradeva (who built the Thousand Pillar temple, 1163), peaked under Ganapatideva (1199–1262) and Rudramadevi (1262–1289), and fell in 1323. They united Telugu lands, built tanks and promoted trade through Motupalli.',
revise:['Feudatories of Kalyani Chalukyas → Chalukya decline → independence under Rudradeva (Thousand Pillar temple, 1163).','Ganapatideva unites Telugu lands → capital Orugallu → tanks (Ramappa, Pakhal) → agricultural prosperity.','Trade policy (Motupalli inscription, lower duties) → overseas trade → wealth.','Rudramadevi’s rule → challenges from nobles and neighbours → overcome → Marco Polo’s praise.','Delhi Sultanate’s southern campaigns (1309–10, 1323) → tribute → conquest by Ulugh Khan → end of Kakatiyas.','Fall → Nayaka resistance → Musunuri recapture Warangal (c. 1336).'],
analysis:['The Kakatiyas were the first to unite almost all Telugu-speaking areas, creating a sense of Telugu identity.','Nayankara system: military chiefs held land in return for troops, which gave strength but later local power.','Rudramadevi is a rare example of a woman ruler in medieval India; her success shows that capable rule could overcome social prejudice.','Tank irrigation shows state-supported agrarian expansion, still relevant in Telangana’s Mission Kakatiya.'],
mind:['Kakatiyas',[['Rise',['Beta I, Prola I','Prola II','Rudradeva 1163']],['Peak',['Ganapatideva','Rudramadevi','Orugallu']],['Economy',['Tanks','Motupalli trade','Nayankara']],['Culture',['Thousand Pillar','Ramappa 1213','Kakatiya Thoranam']],['Fall',['Malik Kafur 1310','Ulugh Khan 1323']]]],
table:['Kakatiya rulers',['Ruler','Years','Remember'],[['Prola II','d. 1158','Last feudatory'],['Rudradeva','1158–1195','Independence; Thousand Pillar temple 1163'],['Mahadeva','1195–1199','Brief'],['Ganapatideva','1199–1262','Orugallu; Motupalli; Ramappa built by his general'],['Rudramadevi','1262–1289','Woman ruler; Marco Polo'],['Prataparudra II','1289–1323','Defeated by Ulugh Khan']]],
places:[['Hanumakonda',79.55,18.0],['Warangal (Orugallu)',79.58,17.97],['Ramappa',79.94,18.26],['Pakhal lake',80.08,17.95],['Motupalli',80.1,15.75],['Devagiri',75.21,19.94]],
figs:['map_8.2'],
qa:[['Evaluate the reign of Rudramadevi.',150,'Rudramadevi (1262–1289) was one of the few women rulers in medieval India. Ganapatideva, having no son, trained her and made her co-ruler and successor. She ruled under the male title Rudradeva Maharaja. Some nobles and relatives resisted rule by a woman, and neighbours attacked: the Yadavas of Devagiri invaded, and she defeated them; she also faced the Eastern Gangas and Kayasthas. With loyal generals like Gona Gannareddi and Recharla Prasaditya, she kept the kingdom intact. She continued tank building and support for trade. The Venetian traveller Marco Polo, who visited Motupalli, praised her justice and her people’s prosperity. She is said to have died fighting a rebel chief. Her success shows that capable leadership could overcome social prejudice. Her reign shows that capable rule could overcome social prejudice, and she remains a symbol of courage and good governance in Telugu history.'],
['Discuss the economic and cultural achievements of the Kakatiyas.',250,'The Kakatiyas (independent from about 1163 to 1323) ruled from Hanumakonda and Orugallu and were the first dynasty to unite most Telugu-speaking lands. Their economic and cultural achievements were significant.\n\nEconomically, their greatest contribution was irrigation. Kings and nobles built large tanks such as those at Ramappa, Pakhal and Laknavaram, and thousands of village tanks. These turned the dry Telangana plateau into productive farmland, supporting rice and other crops. Land grants and the extension of cultivation increased revenue. Trade was encouraged: Ganapatideva’s Motupalli inscription promised fair duties to foreign merchants and protection from shipwreck losses, which attracted overseas trade. Crafts such as textiles and metalwork flourished, and Warangal carpets were famous. The nayankara system, under which military chiefs held land in return for troops, organised the state’s resources.\n\nCulturally, the Kakatiyas created a distinctive style of architecture. The Thousand Pillar temple at Hanumakonda (1163) and the Ramappa temple (1213), built by Ganapatideva’s general Recharla Rudra and now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, have star-shaped platforms, finely polished black basalt pillars and lively sculptures of dancers and musicians. The Kakatiya Thoranam, the ornamental gateway of Warangal fort, is now a state symbol.\n\nIn religion, they supported Shaivism, especially the Pashupata and Kalamukha sects, but also tolerated others. Literature in Sanskrit and Telugu grew; Tikkana completed much of the Telugu Mahabharata in this age, and works like Jayapa’s Nritta Ratnavali on dance were written.\n\nThus the Kakatiyas left a legacy of irrigation, trade, architecture and Telugu identity that long outlived their kingdom.']]});

X('ap-reddi',{lesson:'AP sources; NIOS Lesson 11 (regional states after Sultanate decline)',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS explains that regional states rose as the Delhi Sultanate weakened, which fits Andhra after 1323. Telugu chiefs led by Musunuri Prolaya Nayaka and Kapaya Nayaka drove out the Tughlaq governors and recaptured Warangal (about 1336). On the coast, Prolaya Vema Reddi founded the Reddi kingdom (1325), first at Addanki and then Kondaveedu; Kumaragiri Reddi and Pedakomati Vema Reddi patronised arts, and Srinatha was court poet. The Recharla Velamas ruled from Rachakonda and Devarakonda and defeated Kapaya Nayaka in 1368. These kingdoms were later absorbed by the Gajapatis, Vijayanagara and the Bahmanis.',
revise:['Kakatiya fall (1323) → Tughlaq rule → Nayaka resistance → Warangal recaptured (c. 1336).','Coastal chiefs → Reddi kingdom (1325) → Addanki → Kondaveedu.','Reddi patronage → Srinatha, arts, temples → Telugu literature flourishes.','Musunuri vs Velamas → Bhimavaram (1368) → Velama power in Telangana.','Pressure from Vijayanagara, Bahmanis and Gajapatis → Reddi and Velama kingdoms absorbed (15th century).'],
analysis:['The Nayaka resistance shows that local leaders could unite against outside rule, a theme for Telugu identity.','The Reddi age was culturally rich despite constant warfare, showing the importance of regional courts.','Rivalries among Telugu kingdoms weakened them and allowed larger powers to absorb them.'],
mind:['After the Kakatiyas',[['Musunuri',['Prolaya Nayaka','Kapaya Nayaka','Warangal c. 1336']],['Reddis',['Prolaya Vema','Kumaragiri','Pedakomati','Kondaveedu']],['Velamas',['Rachakonda','Anapota','Sarvajna Singabhupala']],['Literature',['Srinatha','Rasarnava Sudhakara']],['End',['Vijayanagara','Bahmanis','Gajapatis']]]],
table:['Telugu kingdoms after 1323',['Kingdom','Capital','Key rulers','Remember'],[['Musunuri Nayakas','Warangal','Prolaya, Kapaya','Recaptured Warangal'],['Reddis (Kondaveedu)','Addanki, Kondaveedu','Prolaya Vema, Kumaragiri, Pedakomati','Srinatha'],['Reddis (Rajahmundry)','Rajahmundry','Kataya Vema, Allaya Vema','Till c. 1448'],['Recharla Velamas','Rachakonda, Devarakonda','Anapota, Sarvajna Singabhupala','Defeated Kapaya 1368']]],
places:[['Warangal',79.58,17.97],['Addanki',79.98,15.81],['Kondaveedu',80.25,16.25],['Rajahmundry',81.78,17.0],['Rachakonda',78.88,17.13],['Devarakonda',78.93,16.69]],
figs:[],
qa:[['Describe the role of the Musunuri Nayakas.',150,'After Ulugh Khan conquered Warangal in 1323, the Delhi Sultanate ruled Telangana through governors. Local Telugu chiefs, the nayakas who had served the Kakatiyas, resented this rule. Under the leadership of Prolaya Nayaka of the Musunuri family, they united and rose against the Tughlaq officers. His cousin Kapaya Nayaka continued the struggle and, with help from Hoysala Ballala III and other chiefs, recaptured Warangal around 1336, ending Delhi’s control. Kapaya Nayaka ruled Telangana until 1368, when he was defeated and killed by the Recharla Velamas at Bhimavaram. The Musunuri Nayakas are remembered for restoring Telugu independence after the Kakatiya fall and for inspiring the foundation of other independent Hindu kingdoms in the south. Their revolt shows how local leaders could unite against outside rule, and it encouraged the founding of Vijayanagara in 1336.'],
['Assess the cultural achievements of the Reddi kingdom.',250,'The Reddi kingdom, founded by Prolaya Vema Reddi in 1325 with its capital first at Addanki and later Kondaveedu, ruled coastal Andhra for about a century. Despite constant wars with the Velamas, Bahmanis, Vijayanagara and Gajapatis, it achieved much in culture.\n\nIts greatest achievement was in literature. The Reddi court was a centre of Telugu and Sanskrit learning. Srinatha, called Kavisarvabhauma, was the court poet of Pedakomati Vema Reddi; his works, such as Sringara Naishadham, Kasi Khandam and Bhimakhandam, are classics, and his travels and verses give vivid pictures of society. Errana, who completed the Telugu Mahabharata begun by Nannaya and continued by Tikkana, also belonged to this period. Kumaragiri Reddi was himself a scholar, linked with works on dance and music, and the kings patronised Sanskrit scholars.\n\nIn religion and architecture, the Reddis built and repaired temples and supported Shaiva and Vaishnava centres, including Srisailam and Ahobilam. Forts like Kondaveedu and Kondapalli show their military architecture.\n\nThe arts flourished. Kumaragiri’s court celebrated Vasantotsavam (spring festivals) with music and dance, and the Reddi period contributed to classical dance traditions that later influenced Kuchipudi.\n\nEconomically, they supported agriculture and trade through ports such as Motupalli, providing resources for patronage.\n\nThe Reddi kingdom was absorbed by the Gajapatis and Vijayanagara in the 15th century, but its literary and cultural contributions, especially Srinatha’s poetry, remain an important part of Telugu heritage.']]});

X('ap-vijay',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 11: Emergence of Regional States (Vijayanagara and Bahmani)',
brief:'NIOS says the Vijayanagara kingdom was founded in 1336 by Harihara and Bukka, brothers who had served Muhammad bin Tughlaq and broke away. Between 1336 and 1565 it was ruled by the Sangama (till 1485), Saluva (till 1503) and Tuluva dynasties. NIOS calls Krishnadevaraya the founder of the Tuluva dynasty (most books give Vira Narasimha as founder and Krishnadevaraya as its greatest king); he built fine stone temples and added impressive gopurams, and after him Achyuta Deva Raya ruled from 1530. Vijayanagara used firearms and employed Turkish and Portuguese experts; Devaraya II enrolled Muslims in his army. Both Vijayanagara and the Bahmanis could expand only across the Tungabhadra, so they fought over the Raichur doab; the Bahmanis reconquered it in 1504. Bankers and moneylenders (mahajans) grew influential. The empire is now part of Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.',
revise:['Tughlaq decline → Harihara and Bukka found Vijayanagara (1336) with Vidyaranya’s support.','Raichur doab and trade routes → long wars with Bahmanis → large army, firearms, foreign experts.','Krishnadevaraya’s victories (Raichur, Gajapatis) + strong administration (nayankara) → peak of empire.','Royal patronage → Telugu golden age (Ashtadiggajas), temple gopurams, Hampi.','Deccan Sultanates unite → Talikota (1565) → Hampi destroyed → Aravidu rule from Penukonda and Chandragiri.'],
analysis:['Vijayanagara is often called a “Hindu empire”, but its army and administration included Muslims, and its conflicts were about land and trade more than religion.','The nayankara system made the empire militarily strong but gave power to nayakas, who later became independent (Madurai, Thanjavur, Gingee).','Talikota did not end the empire immediately; it continued under the Aravidus for about 80 years, with Rayalaseema (Penukonda, Chandragiri) as its base, a key AP link.'],
mind:['Vijayanagara',[['Dynasties',['Sangama','Saluva','Tuluva','Aravidu']],['Krishnadevaraya',['Raichur','Gajapatis','Amuktamalyada','Ashtadiggajas']],['Administration',['Nayankara','Ayagar','Mahajans']],['Wars',['Bahmanis','Raichur doab','Talikota 1565']],['Culture',['Hampi','Gopurams','Lepakshi','Telugu golden age']]]],
table:['Vijayanagara dynasties (NIOS and standard)',['Dynasty','Years','Key ruler'],[['Sangama','1336–1485','Harihara, Bukka, Devaraya II'],['Saluva','1485–1503/05','Saluva Narasimha'],['Tuluva','1505–1570','Krishnadevaraya (1509–29), Achyuta (from 1530), Sadasiva'],['Aravidu','1570–c. 1646','Tirumala, Venkata II']]],
places:[['Vijayanagara (Hampi)',76.46,15.33],['Raichur',77.36,16.2],['Talikota',76.32,16.47],['Penukonda',77.6,14.08],['Chandragiri',79.31,13.59],['Lepakshi',77.6,13.8],['Tirupati',79.42,13.63],['Gulbarga (Bahmani)',76.83,17.33]],
figs:[],
qa:[['Explain the nayankara system of Vijayanagara.',150,'The nayankara system was the military and administrative backbone of the Vijayanagara empire. The king granted territories called amaram to military chiefs known as nayakas or amaranayakas. In return, a nayaka collected revenue from the territory, maintained a fixed number of soldiers, horses and elephants for the king’s army, and paid a part of the revenue to the treasury. Nayakas also maintained irrigation works and temples in their areas. The system allowed the empire to raise large armies quickly, which was vital in wars against the Bahmanis and Deccan Sultanates. However, nayakas gradually became powerful and hereditary, and after Talikota (1565) many, such as those of Madurai, Thanjavur and Gingee, became independent rulers. The system thus combined military strength with local control, but it also weakened the centre over time.'],
['Assess Krishnadevaraya as a ruler and patron of culture.',250,'Krishnadevaraya (1509–1529) of the Tuluva dynasty is regarded as the greatest ruler of Vijayanagara. NIOS describes him as one of its most important rulers and notes his temple building.\n\nAs a military leader he was highly successful. He defeated the Bahmani successor states, captured Raichur in 1520 and strengthened the empire’s hold over the Raichur doab. He campaigned against the Gajapatis of Odisha, capturing forts like Udayagiri and Kondaveedu, and later made peace by marrying a Gajapati princess. He kept friendly relations with the Portuguese to secure horses for his cavalry.\n\nAs an administrator, he strengthened the nayankara system, built irrigation tanks and canals, and encouraged trade. His Telugu work Amuktamalyada contains advice on statecraft: the king should protect subjects, encourage trade and ports, and rule with justice. Foreign visitors such as Domingo Paes and Fernao Nuniz praised the prosperity and splendour of his capital.\n\nAs a patron of culture, he was outstanding. His court had the Ashtadiggajas, eight great poets, including Allasani Peddana, called Andhra Kavita Pitamaha, and Tenali Ramakrishna. This was the golden age of Telugu prabandha literature, and he earned the title Andhra Bhoja. He also patronised Sanskrit, Kannada and Tamil writers. He built and enlarged temples, adding tall gopurams at Hampi, Tirupati, Srisailam and other shrines, as NIOS notes.\n\nHis limitations included heavy reliance on nayakas and a disputed succession after his death. Still, his reign marked the military, administrative and cultural peak of Vijayanagara and of medieval Telugu culture.']]});

X('ap-qutb',{lesson:'NIOS Lessons 10–11 (Golconda and the Deccan Sultanates; Mughal annexation) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS notes that the Bahmani kingdom split into five Deccan Sultanates, including Golconda, that Shah Jahan signed treaties with Bijapur and Golconda in 1636, and that by 1687 Aurangzeb annexed both. Its regional lessons also mention Golconda’s diamonds. The Qutb Shahis, founded by Sultan Quli (1518), ruled most of Andhra and Telangana from Golconda; Ibrahim Qutb Shah patronised Telugu poets; Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah founded Hyderabad and built Charminar (1591); the last ruler Abul Hasan Tana Shah, with ministers Madanna and Akkanna, lost the kingdom to Aurangzeb in 1687.',
revise:['Bahmani split → Golconda kingdom under Sultan Quli (1518).','Rich lands + diamonds (Kollur) + Masulipatnam port → wealthy state.','Patronage of Telugu and Persian → composite Deccani culture (Malki Bharama, Urdu poetry).','Water and population needs → Hyderabad founded (1591), Charminar.','Mughal pressure → treaty (1636) → annexation by Aurangzeb (1687).'],
analysis:['The Qutb Shahis show a composite culture: Persian court with Telugu officials and poets; Hindu ministers like Madanna and Akkanna held high office.','Golconda’s wealth from diamonds and textile exports made it a target for the Mughals.','Hyderabad’s founding created a lasting urban centre that shaped Telangana’s and Andhra’s later history.'],
mind:['Qutb Shahis',[['Rulers',['Sultan Quli','Ibrahim','Muhammad Quli','Abdullah','Tana Shah']],['Economy',['Kollur diamonds','Masulipatnam','Textiles']],['Culture',['Telugu poets','Urdu','Charminar','Golconda fort']],['Fall',['1636 treaty','1687 Aurangzeb']]]],
table:['Qutb Shahi rulers',['Ruler','Years','Remember'],[['Sultan Quli','1518–1543','Founder'],['Ibrahim Qutb Shah','1550–1580','Malki Bharama; Telugu patron'],['Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah','1580–1612','Hyderabad, Charminar 1591'],['Abdullah Qutb Shah','1626–1672','Accepted Mughal overlordship (1636)'],['Abul Hasan Tana Shah','1672–1687','Madanna, Akkanna; annexed 1687']]],
places:[['Golconda',78.4,17.38],['Hyderabad',78.47,17.38],['Masulipatnam',81.13,16.19],['Kollur (diamonds)',80.05,16.18],['Bhadrachalam',80.89,17.67]],
figs:['map_10.3'],
qa:[['What was the cultural contribution of the Qutb Shahis?',150,'The Qutb Shahis of Golconda (1518–1687) created a rich composite culture. Ibrahim Qutb Shah, whom Telugu poets called Malki Bharama, patronised Telugu poets such as Addanki Gangadhara. Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah was himself a poet who wrote in Deccani Urdu, Telugu and Persian, and founded Hyderabad in 1591, building the Charminar. Their architecture blended Persian and Indian styles: Golconda fort, the Qutb Shahi tombs, Mecca Masjid and Charminar. Hindu ministers such as Madanna and Akkanna served under Tana Shah, and Kancherla Gopanna (Bhakta Ramadasu) built the Bhadrachalam temple. Golconda’s diamonds and Masulipatnam’s painted textiles were famous worldwide. This Telugu–Persian synthesis shaped the culture of Hyderabad and Andhra. Their buildings and composite traditions remain central to Hyderabad’s identity today, and Golconda fort is one of India’s most visited monuments.'],
['Explain the rise and fall of the Qutb Shahi kingdom of Golconda.',250,'The Qutb Shahi kingdom emerged from the break-up of the Bahmani kingdom, which, as NIOS notes, split into five Deccan Sultanates.\n\nSultan Quli, a Persian noble who governed Telangana for the Bahmanis, declared independence in 1518 and made the fort of Golconda his capital. His successors expanded east to the coast, taking over much of coastal Andhra from the Gajapatis and Vijayanagara. Ibrahim Qutb Shah (1550–80) joined the other Sultanates against Vijayanagara at Talikota in 1565, which removed a major rival.\n\nThe kingdom’s strength rested on rich agricultural land in the deltas, the famous diamond mines at Kollur, and the port of Masulipatnam, which exported textiles and attracted Dutch and English traders. Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah founded Hyderabad in 1591 to relieve crowding at Golconda, and it grew into a great city. The rulers employed Telugu officials and patronised Telugu, Persian and Urdu culture.\n\nDecline came from Mughal pressure. As NIOS records, Shah Jahan forced treaties on Bijapur and Golconda in 1636, under which Golconda accepted Mughal overlordship and paid tribute. Internal rivalries, the growing power of ministers, and the kingdom’s wealth attracted Mughal interest. The last ruler, Abul Hasan Tana Shah, relied on his Brahmin ministers Madanna and Akkanna and allied with the Marathas, which angered Aurangzeb.\n\nAurangzeb besieged Golconda, and in 1687 the fort fell through treachery after a long siege. Tana Shah was imprisoned, and Golconda became part of the Mughal empire. Mughal rule lasted until 1724, when Nizam-ul-Mulk founded the Asaf Jahi state.']]});

X('ap-asaf',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 15 (Nizam-ul-Mulk founds Hyderabad, 1724) and Lesson 16 (subsidiary alliance, Hyderabad 1798)',
brief:'NIOS records that Nizam-ul-Mulk, having failed to reform the Mughal administration, gave up his office in 1723 and in October 1724 marched south to establish the state of Hyderabad. In its British-rule lesson, NIOS lists Hyderabad (1798) among the princely states created by subsidiary alliances. From AP sources: the Northern Circars passed to the French (1753) and then to the British (Mughal grant of 1765, treaties of 1766 and 1768); Nizam Ali Khan ceded the districts of Bellary, Cuddapah, Kurnool and Anantapur (the Ceded Districts, later Rayalaseema) in 1800 to pay for subsidiary troops. Hyderabad State remained under the Nizams until Operation Polo in 1948.',
revise:['Mughal decline → Nizam-ul-Mulk founds Hyderabad state (1724).','Succession war after 1748 → French and British take sides → Northern Circars to the French (1753) → to the British (1765–68).','Subsidiary Alliance (1798) → British troops in Hyderabad → payment by ceding districts (1800) → Ceded Districts / Rayalaseema.','Andhra divided: coast and Rayalaseema under British Madras Presidency; Telangana under the Nizam.','Different rulers → different development and identities → later regional movements.'],
analysis:['Hyderabad is a classic successor state: founded by a Mughal governor, keeping Mughal forms.','The cession of Andhra districts to the British explains why coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema had British administration while Telangana remained under the Nizam, a key fact for 1956 and 2014.'],
mind:['Asaf Jahis',[['Founding',['Nizam-ul-Mulk','1724']],['Losses',['Northern Circars 1765–68','Ceded Districts 1800']],['British',['Subsidiary Alliance 1798','Resident']],['Later Nizams',['Salar Jung I','Mir Osman Ali Khan']],['End',['Operation Polo 1948']]]],
table:['How Andhra regions passed to the British',['Region','Year','How'],[['Northern Circars (coastal Andhra)','1765–1768','Mughal grant, treaties with the Nizam'],['Guntur Circar','1788','Transferred by the Nizam'],['Ceded Districts (Bellary, Cuddapah, Kurnool, Anantapur)','1800','Ceded for subsidiary troops'],['Telangana','—','Remained with the Nizam until 1948']]],
places:[['Hyderabad',78.47,17.38],['Aurangabad',75.34,19.88],['Rajahmundry (Circars)',81.78,17.0],['Bellary',76.92,15.14],['Kadapa',78.82,14.47],['Kurnool',78.04,15.83],['Anantapur',77.6,14.68]],
figs:['map_16.2'],
qa:[['How did the Ceded Districts come under British rule?',150,'In 1798 the Nizam of Hyderabad, Nizam Ali Khan, became the first Indian ruler to accept Lord Wellesley’s Subsidiary Alliance, as NIOS notes. He had to keep British troops in his state and pay for them. After the defeat of Tipu Sultan in 1799, the Nizam received part of Mysore’s territory. In 1800, to pay permanently for the subsidiary force, he ceded these and other districts, Bellary, Cuddapah (Kadapa), Kurnool and Anantapur, to the British. These became known as the Ceded Districts and were placed under Thomas Munro, who introduced the ryotwari system. In 1928, at a conference in Nandyal, the region was renamed Rayalaseema, the land of the Rayas, recalling its Vijayanagara past. The name recalls the glory of Vijayanagara rulers like Krishnadevaraya and remains a strong regional identity in Andhra Pradesh today.'],
['Explain how Andhra came to be divided between British India and the Nizam’s dominions.',250,'In the 18th century, the Telugu-speaking lands came under two different rulers, British India and the Nizam of Hyderabad, and this division shaped their later history.\n\nThe process began with the founding of the Asaf Jahi state. As NIOS notes, Nizam-ul-Mulk left the Mughal court and in 1724 established Hyderabad in the Deccan. His state included most of Telangana, coastal Andhra and parts of Rayalaseema.\n\nAfter his death in 1748, a war of succession broke out, and the French and British supported rival claimants. The French under Bussy helped Salabat Jung and in return received the Northern Circars, the coastal districts of Andhra, in 1753. After the British defeated the French, Clive obtained a Mughal grant for the Circars in 1765, and treaties with the Nizam in 1766 and 1768 confirmed British control. The Guntur Circar followed in 1788.\n\nThe next stage came through the Subsidiary Alliance. In 1798 the Nizam accepted British troops, and in 1800 he ceded Bellary, Cuddapah, Kurnool and Anantapur to pay for them. These Ceded Districts later became Rayalaseema.\n\nAs a result, coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema became part of the British Madras Presidency, with zamindari and ryotwari systems, English education, irrigation works such as the Godavari and Krishna anicuts, and a growing middle class. Telangana remained under the Nizam’s feudal administration, with Urdu as the official language and fewer modern institutions.\n\nThis division created different political, economic and cultural experiences, which explains the separate Andhra movement in Madras Presidency, the Telangana struggle in Hyderabad State, and the regional tensions that followed the formation of Andhra Pradesh in 1956.']]});

X('ap-british',{lesson:'NIOS Lessons 16–17 (British rule, revenue systems) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS explains British revenue systems: the Permanent Settlement of 1793 and the ryotwari system. In Andhra, the Permanent Settlement applied to the Northern Circars (1802), while Thomas Munro introduced ryotwari in the Ceded Districts. NIOS also notes that Masulipatnam declined as colonial port cities rose. Other key events: the English factory at Masulipatnam (1611), Golconda’s “Golden Farman” (1632), the battle of Bobbili (1757), Uyyalawada Narasimha Reddy’s revolt (1846), and Sir Arthur Cotton’s Dowleswaram anicut (1852) and the Krishna anicut (1855), which transformed delta agriculture.',
revise:['Trade at Masulipatnam (1611) + Golden Farman (1632) → English foothold in Andhra.','French–English rivalry → Bobbili (1757) → Northern Circars to British (1765–68).','Revenue systems → zamindari in Circars, ryotwari in Ceded Districts → heavy demand → famines and revolts.','Famines (e.g. Guntur famine 1832–33) → irrigation works → Cotton’s anicuts → delta prosperity.','Western education, missionaries, press → new middle class → social reform and nationalism.'],
analysis:['British rule had mixed effects: irrigation made the deltas rich, while Rayalaseema suffered from famines and heavy revenue.','Arthur Cotton is respected in Andhra for the Godavari anicut, showing that colonial projects could bring lasting benefits, even if made for revenue.','Local revolts (Uyyalawada, Rampa) show resistance before 1857.'],
mind:['British rule in Andhra',[['Trade',['Masulipatnam 1611','Golden Farman 1632']],['Conquest',['Bobbili 1757','Circars 1765–68','Ceded 1800']],['Revenue',['Zamindari 1802','Ryotwari – Munro']],['Irrigation',['Dowleswaram 1852','Krishna 1855']],['Resistance',['Uyyalawada 1846','Rampa 1879']]]],
table:['British rule in Andhra: key events',['Year','Event'],[['1611','English factory at Masulipatnam'],['1632','Golden Farman from Golconda'],['1757','Battle of Bobbili'],['1765–68','Northern Circars to British'],['1800','Ceded Districts'],['1802','Permanent Settlement in Circars'],['1846','Uyyalawada Narasimha Reddy’s revolt'],['1852 / 1855','Godavari and Krishna anicuts']]],
places:[['Masulipatnam',81.13,16.19],['Bobbili',83.36,18.57],['Vizianagaram',83.4,18.11],['Dowleswaram',81.79,16.95],['Vijayawada',80.65,16.51],['Uyyalawada',78.37,15.13],['Guntur',80.44,16.3]],
figs:[],
qa:[['What was the contribution of Sir Arthur Cotton to Andhra?',150,'Sir Arthur Cotton was a British irrigation engineer who transformed agriculture in coastal Andhra. After severe famines such as the Guntur famine of 1832–33, he planned an anicut (weir) across the Godavari at Dowleswaram, completed in 1852, with a network of canals for irrigation and navigation. The Krishna anicut at Vijayawada followed in 1855. These works turned the Godavari and Krishna deltas into some of the most fertile rice-growing areas in India, ended frequent famines there and increased population and prosperity. Canals also served as waterways for trade. Although the British built them to secure revenue, the people of the delta remember Cotton with gratitude; his statues stand in the region and some farmers honour him in festivals. The Dowleswaram anicut was rebuilt as the Sir Arthur Cotton Barrage in the 1970s, and delta agriculture still depends on this system.'],
['Assess the impact of British rule on Andhra.',250,'British rule in Andhra, from the acquisition of the Northern Circars (1765–68) and the Ceded Districts (1800) to independence, had mixed effects.\n\nEconomically, land revenue systems were harsh. The Permanent Settlement of 1802 in the Circars strengthened zamindars, who often oppressed tenants. In the Ceded Districts, Munro’s ryotwari system made peasants pay heavy cash revenue directly to the state. Revenue pressure, debt and droughts led to famines, such as the Guntur famine of 1832–33 and the great famine of 1876–78, which hit Rayalaseema badly. Traditional industries such as Masulipatnam’s textiles declined with British machine goods and the rise of Madras as a port, as NIOS describes for old ports generally.\n\nAt the same time, irrigation projects such as Cotton’s Dowleswaram (1852) and Krishna (1855) anicuts made the deltas prosperous, creating a wealthy farming class that later invested in education, business and politics. Railways and roads improved connections.\n\nSocially and culturally, English education, missionary schools and colleges created a modern educated class in towns like Rajahmundry, Machilipatnam and Guntur. The press grew, and reformers like Kandukuri Veeresalingam fought social evils, leading to a Telugu renaissance.\n\nPolitically, British rule provoked resistance, from Uyyalawada Narasimha Reddy (1846) to the Rampa rebellions, and later Andhra’s strong participation in the national movement. The experience of being a minority within Madras Presidency also gave rise to the Andhra movement for a separate state.\n\nOverall, British rule impoverished many, especially in Rayalaseema, but it also brought irrigation, education and new ideas that shaped modern Andhra.']]});

X('ap-reform',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 18 (social reform, education, press) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS’s lesson on social change describes reform movements, women’s issues like widow remarriage, English education and the press. In Andhra these ideas produced a Telugu renaissance. Kandukuri Veeresalingam arranged the first widow remarriage in Andhra at Rajahmundry (11 December 1881), started the journal Viveka Vardhini and wrote Rajasekhara Charitra. Gurajada Apparao’s play Kanyasulkam (1892) attacked bride price. Gidugu Ramamurthy led the movement for spoken Telugu in literature and education. Raghupati Venkataratnam Naidu led the Brahmo movement, Komarraju Lakshmana Rao founded the Vignana Chandrika Mandali, and Durgabai Deshmukh founded the Andhra Mahila Sabha (1937).',
revise:['English education + press + Brahmo ideas → reform leaders in Andhra.','Child marriage and widows’ suffering → Veeresalingam’s widow remarriages (1881) and girls’ schools.','Social satire in literature (Kanyasulkam, 1892) → public opinion against bride price.','Gidugu’s spoken Telugu movement → education and literature reach common people.','Reform + education → new middle class → Andhra movement and nationalism.'],
analysis:['Andhra’s reform movement followed Bengal’s Brahmo influence but had its own leaders and literature.','Reformers faced strong opposition, even social boycott, which shows how hard social change was.','The language movement (Gidugu) was both social reform and the basis of Telugu identity.'],
mind:['Telugu renaissance',[['Reformers',['Veeresalingam','Raghupati Venkataratnam','Komarraju']],['Women',['Widow remarriage 1881','Durgabai Deshmukh']],['Literature',['Kanyasulkam 1892','Rajasekhara Charitra','Malapalli']],['Language',['Gidugu','Spoken Telugu']],['Institutions',['Viveka Vardhini','Vignana Chandrika Mandali']]]],
table:['Telugu reformers',['Reformer','Contribution'],[['Kandukuri Veeresalingam','Widow remarriage (1881); Viveka Vardhini; Rajasekhara Charitra'],['Gurajada Apparao','Kanyasulkam (1892)'],['Gidugu Ramamurthy','Spoken Telugu movement; Telugu Language Day (29 Aug)'],['Raghupati Venkataratnam Naidu','Brahmo leader; social purity'],['Komarraju Lakshmana Rao','Vignana Chandrika Mandali'],['Gurram Jashuva, Unnava Lakshminarayana','Literature against caste (Malapalli)'],['Durgabai Deshmukh','Andhra Mahila Sabha (1937)']]],
places:[['Rajahmundry',81.78,17.0],['Vizianagaram',83.4,18.11],['Kakinada',82.24,16.99],['Machilipatnam',81.13,16.19],['Parlakimidi (Gidugu)',84.1,18.78],['Guntur',80.44,16.3]],
figs:['fig_18.2'],
qa:[['Evaluate the contribution of Kandukuri Veeresalingam to social reform.',150,'Kandukuri Veeresalingam (1848–1919) is called the father of the Telugu renaissance. Inspired by Brahmo ideas and Vidyasagar’s work, he campaigned for widow remarriage and arranged the first such marriage in Andhra at Rajahmundry on 11 December 1881, facing fierce opposition and social boycott. He founded schools for girls and homes for widows and supported women’s education. Through his journal Viveka Vardhini (1874) he attacked corruption, superstition and social evils. He pioneered modern Telugu prose, writing Rajasekhara Charitra, one of the first Telugu novels, plays and an autobiography, earning the title Gadya Tikkana. He founded the Hitakarini Samaj to continue his work. His courage made Rajahmundry a centre of reform and inspired later generations in Andhra. His statues and the institutions he founded at Rajahmundry, such as the Hitakarini Samaj, keep his memory and his message of reform alive today.'],
['Discuss the Telugu renaissance of the late 19th and early 20th centuries.',250,'The Telugu renaissance was a period of social reform, literary creativity and new awareness in Andhra, influenced by the wider reform movements that NIOS describes, English education and the press.\n\nIn social reform, Kandukuri Veeresalingam was the central figure. He campaigned against child marriage, the plight of widows and corruption, arranged widow remarriages from 1881, and opened girls’ schools. Raghupati Venkataratnam Naidu spread Brahmo ideas and worked for social purity and the uplift of depressed classes. Later, Durgabai Deshmukh founded the Andhra Mahila Sabha (1937) for women’s welfare.\n\nIn literature, writers used new forms to criticise society. Gurajada Apparao’s play Kanyasulkam (1892) satirised bride price and orthodoxy in spoken Telugu, and his poem Desamunu preminchumanna gave a humane idea of patriotism. Veeresalingam wrote early novels and prose works. Unnava Lakshminarayana’s Malapalli and Gurram Jashuva’s poetry attacked caste discrimination.\n\nIn language, Gidugu Ramamurthy led the movement for using spoken Telugu (vyavaharika bhasha) in literature and education, in place of the classical grandhika style. This made learning accessible to ordinary people; his birthday is celebrated as Telugu Language Day.\n\nIn knowledge, Komarraju Lakshmana Rao’s Vignana Chandrika Mandali published books on history and science and began a Telugu encyclopaedia, and many journals spread new ideas.\n\nThe renaissance had limits: it was led by an educated upper-caste elite and faced strong orthodox opposition. Yet it modernised Telugu society, created pride in the Telugu language and history, and prepared the ground for the Andhra movement and participation in the national struggle.']]});

X('ap-national',{lesson:'NIOS Lessons 20–21 (national movement) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'NIOS’s national movement lessons (Swadeshi 1905, non-cooperation, Simon Commission boycott, civil disobedience, Quit India) all had strong Andhra chapters. Bipin Chandra Pal’s 1907 visit sparked the Vandemataram movement; the Andhra Jateeya Kalasala was founded at Machilipatnam (1910); in 1921 came the Chirala–Perala movement (Duggirala Gopalakrishnayya), the Pedanandipadu no-tax campaign and the Palnadu forest satyagraha (Kanneganti Hanumanthu). Alluri Sitarama Raju led the Rampa rebellion (1922–24). Tanguturi Prakasam faced police guns in the 1928 Simon Commission protest in Madras, earning the title Andhra Kesari. Pingali Venkayya designed the flag that became the basis of the national flag.',
revise:['Swadeshi (1905) + Pal’s visit (1907) → Vandemataram movement in Andhra → national education (Machilipatnam 1910).','Non-Cooperation (1920–22) → local forms: Chirala–Perala, Pedanandipadu, Palnadu.','Forest laws + tribal grievances → Rampa rebellion under Alluri Sitarama Raju (1922–24).','Simon Commission boycott (1928) → Prakasam’s courage → Andhra Kesari.','Salt satyagraha and Quit India → mass participation in coastal Andhra.','National movement + Telugu identity → Andhra movement for a separate state.'],
analysis:['Andhra’s participation shows how national programmes took local forms (no-tax, forest satyagraha), a good example for Mains.','Alluri Sitarama Raju’s armed tribal revolt differed from Gandhian non-violence but shared the anti-colonial goal.','The national movement and Telugu linguistic identity grew together and reinforced each other.'],
mind:['Andhra in the national movement',[['Swadeshi',['Pal 1907','Rajahmundry, Kakinada','Machilipatnam 1910']],['Non-Cooperation',['Chirala–Perala','Pedanandipadu','Palnadu']],['Tribal',['Rampa 1922–24','Alluri Sitarama Raju']],['Leaders',['Prakasam','Pingali Venkayya','Konda Venkatappayya']],['Later',['Salt satyagraha','Quit India']]]],
table:['Andhra in the national movement',['Year','Event','Leader'],[['1907','Vandemataram movement','Bipin Chandra Pal’s visit'],['1910','Andhra Jateeya Kalasala','Kopalle Hanumantha Rao'],['1921','Chirala–Perala','Duggirala Gopalakrishnayya'],['1921–22','Pedanandipadu no-tax','Parvataneni Veeraiah Chowdary'],['1922','Palnadu forest satyagraha','Kanneganti Hanumanthu'],['1922–24','Rampa rebellion','Alluri Sitarama Raju'],['1928','Simon boycott','Tanguturi Prakasam']]],
places:[['Rajahmundry',81.78,17.0],['Machilipatnam',81.13,16.19],['Chirala',80.35,15.82],['Pedanandipadu',80.33,16.05],['Palnadu (Macherla)',79.43,16.48],['Rampa',81.78,17.45],['Madras',80.27,13.08],['Kakinada',82.24,16.99]],
figs:['map_21.1'],
qa:[['Describe the Rampa rebellion led by Alluri Sitarama Raju.',150,'The Rampa rebellion of 1922–24 took place in the hilly Agency tracts of the Godavari region. Tribal people there suffered from the Madras Forest Act, which restricted shifting cultivation and forest use, from forced labour on roads, and from exploitation by contractors and officials. Alluri Sitarama Raju, a young sannyasi influenced by the national movement, organised them. Using guerrilla tactics, his followers attacked police stations such as Chintapalli, Krishnadevipeta and Rajavommangi to seize arms. The British sent large forces, including the Malabar Special Police, and spent heavily to suppress the revolt. Raju was captured and shot on 7 May 1924. Although the rebellion failed, it made Alluri Sitarama Raju a hero of Andhra and the national movement. His sacrifice is remembered in films, songs and statues, and the government of India issued a postage stamp in his honour.'],
['Assess Andhra’s contribution to the Indian national movement.',250,'Andhra made a strong and varied contribution to the national movement, taking part in every phase that NIOS describes.\n\nDuring the Swadeshi movement, Bipin Chandra Pal’s speeches in 1907 at Rajahmundry and other towns sparked the Vandemataram movement. Students protested, and the Kakinada and Rajahmundry incidents showed rising anger. National education spread with the Andhra Jateeya Kalasala at Machilipatnam (1910).\n\nIn the Non-Cooperation Movement (1920–22), Andhra developed distinctive local forms. In Chirala–Perala, Duggirala Gopalakrishnayya led the people to leave the town rather than pay a new municipal tax. In Pedanandipadu, peasants refused to pay land revenue in a no-tax campaign. In Palnadu, Kanneganti Hanumanthu led a forest satyagraha and was shot. These showed mass participation of peasants.\n\nAt the same time, Alluri Sitarama Raju led the Rampa rebellion (1922–24), an armed tribal revolt against forest laws and British oppression, which made him a lasting hero.\n\nIn 1928, Tanguturi Prakasam bared his chest to police guns during the Simon Commission protest in Madras, earning the title Andhra Kesari. In the Civil Disobedience Movement, salt satyagraha was held at many coastal places, and Quit India in 1942 saw strikes and attacks on government property.\n\nAndhra also contributed leaders and symbols: Pingali Venkayya designed the flag that became the basis of the national flag, and leaders like Konda Venkatappayya and Pattabhi Sitaramayya worked at the national level.\n\nThe national movement also strengthened Telugu identity, leading to the Andhra movement for a separate state. Thus Andhra’s contribution combined Gandhian mass action, tribal resistance and regional awareness.']]});

X('ap-andhra',{lesson:'AP sources; NIOS Lesson 21 (national movement context)',
brief:AP_NOTE+'Telugu speakers in Madras Presidency felt neglected in education, jobs and development, which led to the Andhra movement. The first Andhra Mahasabha met at Bapatla (1913); a separate Andhra Provincial Congress Committee was formed (1918); the Sri Bagh Pact (16 November 1937) between Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema leaders, signed at Kasinathuni Nageswara Rao’s house in Madras, assured Rayalaseema of fair treatment. After independence, the Dhar Commission and JVP Committee (1948) were cautious. Potti Sriramulu’s fast from 19 October 1952 ended with his death on 15 December 1952, and Andhra State was formed on 1 October 1953 with Kurnool as capital and Tanguturi Prakasam as Chief Minister.',
revise:['Telugu minority in Madras Presidency → neglect in jobs and education → demand for a separate province.','Andhra Mahasabha (1913) + Congress’s linguistic provinces (1920) → organised movement.','Rayalaseema’s fears → Sri Bagh Pact (1937) → unity of Andhra regions.','Post-independence caution (JVP 1948) → Potti Sriramulu’s fast (1952) → death and agitation.','Andhra State (1953) → States Reorganisation Commission → linguistic states across India (1956).'],
analysis:['The Andhra movement was the first successful linguistic state movement, setting the pattern for all of India.','The Sri Bagh Pact shows early recognition of regional imbalance within Andhra, an issue that resurfaced later.','Potti Sriramulu’s sacrifice turned a constitutional demand into mass agitation, forcing Nehru’s decision.'],
mind:['Andhra movement',[['Causes',['Neglect in Madras','Telugu identity']],['Stages',['Bapatla 1913','APCC 1918','Sri Bagh 1937']],['Post-1947',['Dhar Commission','JVP 1948']],['Climax',['Potti Sriramulu','15 Dec 1952']],['Result',['Andhra State 1 Oct 1953','Kurnool','Prakasam']]]],
table:['Andhra movement timeline',['Date','Event'],[['1913','First Andhra Mahasabha, Bapatla'],['1918','Andhra Provincial Congress Committee'],['16 Nov 1937','Sri Bagh Pact'],['1948','Dhar Commission; JVP Committee'],['19 Oct 1952','Potti Sriramulu begins fast'],['15 Dec 1952','Death of Potti Sriramulu'],['1 Oct 1953','Andhra State formed; capital Kurnool']]],
places:[['Bapatla',80.47,15.9],['Madras',80.27,13.08],['Kurnool',78.04,15.83],['Guntur',80.44,16.3],['Nellore',79.98,14.44]],
figs:[],
qa:[['What were the main provisions of the Sri Bagh Pact?',150,'The Sri Bagh Pact was signed on 16 November 1937 between leaders of Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema at “Sri Bagh”, the Madras residence of Kasinathuni Nageswara Rao. Rayalaseema leaders feared domination by the more developed coastal districts in a future Andhra province. The pact addressed their fears. It provided that Andhra University would stay at Waltair and a new university would be set up in Rayalaseema (Anantapur); that the High Court and the capital would be located in different regions, with Rayalaseema getting first choice; that irrigation projects on the Krishna and Tungabhadra would give priority to Rayalaseema’s needs; and that legislative seats would be fairly distributed. The pact united the Andhra regions behind the demand for a separate state, and its principles were later invoked in debates on capitals and development. Its spirit of regional balance continues to be invoked in debates on capitals and development in Andhra Pradesh, including after bifurcation in 2014.'],
['Trace the course of the Andhra movement and its significance for Indian federalism.',250,'The Andhra movement was the struggle of Telugu speakers in Madras Presidency for a separate state, and it shaped India’s federal structure.\n\nIts roots lay in the sense that Telugu people were neglected in education, government jobs and development within a presidency dominated by Madras city. The Telugu renaissance and the press had built pride in language and history. The first Andhra Mahasabha met at Bapatla in 1913, and the Congress accepted the principle of linguistic provinces, forming a separate Andhra Provincial Congress Committee in 1918.\n\nInternal unity was a challenge, as Rayalaseema feared coastal domination. The Sri Bagh Pact of 1937 promised a university for Rayalaseema, the High Court and capital in different regions, and priority in irrigation, uniting the regions.\n\nAfter independence, national leaders hesitated, fearing that linguistic states would weaken unity after partition. The Dhar Commission and the JVP Committee (1948) advised caution. Frustration grew. Potti Sriramulu began a fast unto death on 19 October 1952 demanding Andhra State with Madras as capital. His death on 15 December 1952 caused widespread agitation, and Prime Minister Nehru announced the formation of Andhra State, which came into being on 1 October 1953 with Kurnool as capital and Tanguturi Prakasam as Chief Minister.\n\nIts significance was national. Andhra’s creation led to the States Reorganisation Commission (1953–55) and the States Reorganisation Act of 1956, which reorganised India largely on linguistic lines. This showed that recognising linguistic identity could strengthen democracy and federalism rather than divide the nation. Andhra’s experience also led to the formation of Andhra Pradesh in 1956 by merging with Telangana.']]});

X('ap-hyd',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 15 (Hyderabad state) and AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'Telangana remained under the Nizam’s Hyderabad State, a feudal order with jagirdars and deshmukhs, vetti (forced labour) and Urdu as the official language. Cultural and political organisations grew: the Andhra Jana Sangham (1921), the Andhra Mahasabha in Nizam territory (first conference at Jogipet, 1930), the Arya Samaj and the State Congress. The Telangana Armed Struggle (1946–51), led by the Andhra Mahasabha and Communists (Ravi Narayana Reddy and others), fought landlords and the Nizam. The Razakars under Qasim Razvi terrorised people. Operation Polo (13–17 September 1948) merged Hyderabad with India; Burgula Ramakrishna Rao became its first elected Chief Minister (1952).',
revise:['Nizam’s feudal order + vetti + jagirdars → peasant suffering.','Telugu cultural organisations (Andhra Jana Sangham 1921, Andhra Mahasabha 1930) → political awareness.','Andhra Mahasabha + Communists → armed struggle against landlords (1946–51) → land redistribution.','Nizam’s attempt at independence + Razakar violence → Operation Polo (1948).','Hyderabad State → elections (1952) → merger with Andhra State (1956).'],
analysis:['The Telangana struggle was both an anti-feudal peasant movement and part of the integration of Hyderabad into India.','Operation Polo was necessary to end Razakar violence, but the later suppression of Communist peasants is debated.','Different histories of Telangana and Andhra explain later tensions after 1956.'],
mind:['Hyderabad State',[['Nizam rule',['Feudal','Vetti','Urdu']],['Organisations',['Andhra Jana Sangham 1921','Andhra Mahasabha 1930','State Congress','Arya Samaj']],['Struggle',['1946–51','Ravi Narayana Reddy','Land to tillers']],['Razakars',['Qasim Razvi']],['Integration',['Operation Polo 1948','Burgula 1952']]]],
table:['Hyderabad State timeline',['Year','Event'],[['1921','Andhra Jana Sangham'],['1930','Andhra Mahasabha, Jogipet'],['1938','Hyderabad State Congress'],['1946–51','Telangana Armed Struggle'],['13–17 Sep 1948','Operation Polo'],['1952','Burgula Ramakrishna Rao first elected CM'],['1 Nov 1956','Merged into Andhra Pradesh']]],
places:[['Hyderabad',78.47,17.38],['Jogipet',78.08,17.84],['Nalgonda',79.27,17.05],['Warangal',79.58,17.97],['Khammam',80.15,17.25]],
figs:[],
qa:[['What were the causes of the Telangana Armed Struggle?',150,'The Telangana Armed Struggle (1946–51) arose from the oppressive feudal order in the Nizam’s Hyderabad State. Large landlords (deshmukhs, jagirdars) controlled vast lands and villages and forced peasants into vetti, unpaid labour. Peasants paid high rents and illegal levies, and tenants were evicted at will. The Nizam’s government supported this order and denied civil and political rights, with Urdu as the official language. Organisations such as the Andhra Mahasabha, influenced by Communists, raised awareness among peasants. When the landlords’ agents and the Razakars attacked villagers, peasants took up arms, occupied land and set up village committees. The struggle aimed both to end feudal exploitation and to end the Nizam’s autocratic rule. The struggle forced land reforms and the end of vetti, and it remains an important chapter in the history of peasant movements in India.'],
['Explain the integration of Hyderabad State into the Indian Union.',250,'Hyderabad was the largest princely state in India, ruled by the Nizam Mir Osman Ali Khan. At independence in 1947 the Nizam refused to join India or Pakistan and wanted to remain independent. India signed a Standstill Agreement with him in November 1947, hoping for a peaceful settlement.\n\nSeveral factors made the situation critical. Within the state, popular movements demanded integration and democracy. The Hyderabad State Congress launched a satyagraha, and in Telangana the Andhra Mahasabha and Communists were leading an armed peasant struggle against landlords. At the same time, the Razakars, a militia led by Qasim Razvi of the Majlis, terrorised villages and attacked those who supported integration. The Nizam tried to buy arms abroad and approached the United Nations.\n\nIndia considered an independent Hyderabad in the middle of the country a threat to unity and security. After negotiations failed, the Indian Army launched Operation Polo on 13 September 1948. Within five days, by 17 September, the Nizam’s forces surrendered, the Razakars were disbanded, and the Nizam acceded to India.\n\nHyderabad was placed under a military governor, then a civil administration, and in 1952 the first elections made Burgula Ramakrishna Rao Chief Minister. Land reforms followed, abolishing jagirs.\n\nThe integration ended the Nizam’s autocracy and the feudal order, completing India’s territorial unity. In 1956, Telangana, the Telugu-speaking part of Hyderabad State, merged with Andhra State to form Andhra Pradesh, while other parts went to Bombay and Mysore states.']]});

X('ap-1956',{lesson:'AP sources; NIOS context on linguistic reorganisation',
brief:AP_NOTE+'The States Reorganisation Commission (Fazl Ali) reported in 1955. It noted arguments for and against merging Telangana with Andhra. A Gentlemen’s Agreement (20 February 1956) between Andhra and Telangana leaders promised safeguards for Telangana: a Regional Council, protection of jobs and education through Mulki rules, and spending of Telangana’s surplus revenue in the region. Andhra Pradesh was formed on 1 November 1956 with Hyderabad as capital and Neelam Sanjiva Reddy as the first Chief Minister.',
revise:['Andhra State (1953) + SRC (1953–55) → debate on Vishalandhra (united Telugu state).','Telangana fears of domination → Gentlemen’s Agreement (1956) → safeguards.','Andhra Pradesh formed (1 November 1956) → Hyderabad as capital.','Safeguards not fully implemented → grievances → 1969 agitation.'],
analysis:['Vishalandhra was supported for shared language and economic logic, but the SRC recognised Telangana’s fears; the Gentlemen’s Agreement tried to balance both.','The failure to implement safeguards is a key cause of later movements, a cause-and-effect link for Mains.'],
mind:['Formation of AP',[['Background',['Andhra State 1953','Vishalandhra demand']],['SRC',['Fazl Ali','1955 report']],['Agreement',['20 Feb 1956','Regional Council','Mulki rules']],['Formation',['1 Nov 1956','Hyderabad','Sanjiva Reddy']]]],
table:['Gentlemen’s Agreement (1956)',['Provision','Purpose'],[['Regional Council for Telangana','Oversee development and planning'],['Mulki rules','Jobs for locals'],['Telangana surplus revenue','Spent in Telangana'],['Education facilities','Reserved for Telangana students'],['Cabinet share','Fair representation']]],
places:[['Hyderabad',78.47,17.38],['Kurnool',78.04,15.83]],
figs:[],
qa:[['What were the main provisions of the Gentlemen’s Agreement of 1956?',150,'The Gentlemen’s Agreement was signed on 20 February 1956 by leaders of Andhra and Telangana before the formation of Andhra Pradesh. Telangana leaders feared domination by the more developed and educated Andhra region. The agreement provided safeguards. A Regional Council for Telangana would oversee development, planning and certain subjects. Telangana’s surplus revenue would be spent on its development. Educational facilities and jobs would be reserved for Telangana people, using the existing Mulki rules based on residence. Cabinet positions would be shared fairly, and if the Chief Minister was from Andhra, the Deputy Chief Minister would be from Telangana. Urdu would continue for some years. The non-implementation of many provisions later caused the 1969 Telangana agitation. These safeguards, if fully implemented, might have prevented later conflict, which shows the importance of honouring political agreements.'],
['Why was Andhra Pradesh formed in 1956, and what problems did the merger face?',250,'Andhra Pradesh was formed on 1 November 1956 by merging Andhra State, created in 1953, with the Telugu-speaking Telangana region of Hyderabad State.\n\nThe main argument for the merger was linguistic unity. Telugu speakers had long dreamed of a Vishalandhra, a united Telugu state. A united state was also expected to be economically stronger: the Krishna and Godavari rivers could be developed jointly, and Hyderabad offered Andhra State a well-developed capital instead of Kurnool, which lacked facilities.\n\nThe States Reorganisation Commission under Fazl Ali recognised these advantages, but also noted Telangana’s fears that the more educated and developed Andhra region would dominate jobs, education and land. It even suggested that Telangana could remain separate for some time. To address these fears, leaders signed the Gentlemen’s Agreement on 20 February 1956, providing a Regional Council, protection of jobs through Mulki rules, reserved educational facilities and use of Telangana’s surplus revenue in the region. Andhra Pradesh was then formed with Hyderabad as capital and Neelam Sanjiva Reddy as Chief Minister.\n\nThe merger faced several problems. Many safeguards were not implemented fully: Telangana leaders complained that its surplus revenues were diverted, that Andhra people took government jobs in Telangana, and that the Regional Council was weak. Differences in administrative traditions, education and language usage between the former British and Nizam areas created friction.\n\nThese grievances led to the Telangana agitation of 1969, the Jai Andhra movement of 1972, the Six-Point Formula and Article 371D in 1973, and finally to the separation of Telangana in 2014.']]});

X('ap-after',{lesson:'AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'After 1956, Telangana grievances over jobs, revenue and safeguards led to the 1969 agitation (Telangana Praja Samithi under Marri Chenna Reddy). In 1972 the Supreme Court upheld the Mulki rules, which triggered the Jai Andhra movement in Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema demanding a separate Andhra. The Six-Point Formula (1973) replaced Mulki rules with zonal reservation for jobs and education, given constitutional backing by Article 371D (32nd Amendment, 1973). Politically, the Telugu Desam Party, founded by N.T. Rama Rao in 1982, came to power in 1983 on the theme of Telugu self-respect, and P.V. Narasimha Rao from AP became Prime Minister in 1991.',
revise:['Safeguards not implemented → 1969 Telangana agitation → Telangana Praja Samithi.','Supreme Court upholds Mulki rules (1972) → Andhra anger → Jai Andhra movement.','Both agitations → Six-Point Formula (1973) → zonal system.','Article 371D → local cadres and reservation → constitutional settlement.','Telugu self-respect → TDP (1982) → regional party in power (1983).'],
analysis:['Both Telangana and Jai Andhra movements arose from questions of jobs and fairness, showing that economic issues drove regional politics.','Article 371D is a unique constitutional provision balancing equality with regional protection.','The rise of the TDP marked the arrival of regional parties as major forces in Indian federalism.'],
mind:['AP 1956–2014',[['1969',['Telangana agitation','Mulki rules']],['1972',['Jai Andhra']],['1973',['Six-Point Formula','Article 371D']],['Politics',['TDP 1982','NTR','P.V. Narasimha Rao']]]],
table:['Six-Point Formula (1973) – main points',['Point','Effect'],[['Zonal system','State divided into zones for jobs'],['Local reservation','Preference to local candidates in jobs and education'],['Administrative tribunal','Hear service complaints'],['Abolition of Mulki rules','Replaced by zonal reservation'],['Development of backward areas','Balanced development'],['Constitutional backing','Article 371D']]],
places:[['Hyderabad',78.47,17.38],['Vijayawada',80.65,16.51],['Guntur',80.44,16.3],['Visakhapatnam',83.3,17.7]],
figs:[],
qa:[['What is Article 371D?',150,'Article 371D was added to the Constitution by the 32nd Amendment in 1973 to give legal backing to the Six-Point Formula, which settled the Telangana and Jai Andhra agitations. It allows the President to provide for equitable opportunities and facilities for people of different parts of Andhra Pradesh in public employment and education. Under it, the state was divided into zones, and local cadres were created for certain posts, with preference to local candidates in direct recruitment and in admission to educational institutions. It also provided for an Administrative Tribunal to handle service disputes. After 2014, the provision continues to apply in both Andhra Pradesh and Telangana. It is an example of how the Constitution balances equality with protection for regional interests. It has been invoked often in later debates on jobs and education, including after the 2014 bifurcation.'],
['Examine the causes and consequences of the regional movements in Andhra Pradesh between 1969 and 1973.',250,'Between 1969 and 1973 Andhra Pradesh saw two major regional movements, the Telangana agitation and the Jai Andhra movement, which together threatened its unity.\n\nThe Telangana agitation of 1969 arose because the safeguards of the 1956 Gentlemen’s Agreement were not properly implemented. Telangana people complained that its surplus revenue was spent elsewhere, that the Regional Council was ineffective, and that people from Andhra region took jobs in Telangana in violation of Mulki rules. Students began the protest, and the Telangana Praja Samithi under Marri Chenna Reddy led a mass movement demanding a separate state. Many lives were lost in police firing. The movement won most Telangana Lok Sabha seats in 1971 but then merged with the Congress.\n\nThe Jai Andhra movement of 1972 was a reaction. When the Supreme Court upheld the Mulki rules, people of Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema felt discriminated against in Hyderabad, their capital, and demanded a separate Andhra state. Leaders such as Kakani Venkataratnam led widespread protests.\n\nThe consequences were significant. The central government, under Indira Gandhi, announced the Six-Point Formula in 1973, abolishing Mulki rules and introducing zonal reservation in jobs and education, with an Administrative Tribunal. The 32nd Amendment added Article 371D to give it constitutional backing. President’s Rule was imposed briefly, and P.V. Narasimha Rao had to resign as Chief Minister.\n\nThese measures kept the state united for four decades, but they did not remove underlying regional feelings. The Telangana demand revived after 2001 and led to bifurcation in 2014. The period thus shows how unresolved economic grievances shape regional politics.']]});

X('ap-2014',{lesson:'AP sources',
brief:AP_NOTE+'The Telangana demand revived with the formation of the Telangana Rashtra Samithi (TRS) by K. Chandrashekar Rao in 2001. After agitations, the Srikrishna Committee reported in 2010. Parliament passed the Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act in 2014, and Telangana was formed on 2 June 2014. Hyderabad was made the common capital for up to ten years; the Act promised special help for the residual state, including declaring Polavaram a national project. Residual Andhra Pradesh chose Amaravati as its capital region.',
revise:['Regional grievances unresolved → TRS (2001) → renewed movement.','Agitations and political pressure → Srikrishna Committee (2010) → options studied.','AP Reorganisation Act (2014) → Telangana formed (2 June 2014).','Residual AP loses Hyderabad → capital and revenue challenges → Amaravati, Polavaram, special assistance.'],
analysis:['Bifurcation shows that linguistic unity alone cannot hold a state together if regional development is unequal.','Residual AP’s challenges (capital, revenue deficit, industry) and debates over special category status are current Mains topics.'],
mind:['Bifurcation 2014',[['Movement',['TRS 2001','Agitations']],['Committee',['Srikrishna 2010']],['Act',['AP Reorganisation Act 2014','2 June 2014']],['Provisions',['Common capital 10 years','Polavaram national project']],['Residual AP',['Amaravati','Revenue gap']]]],
table:['AP Reorganisation Act 2014 – key provisions',['Provision','Detail'],[['Appointed day','2 June 2014'],['Common capital','Hyderabad for up to 10 years'],['Polavaram','Declared national project'],['Assets and liabilities','Divided between the states'],['Institutions','New central institutions for residual AP'],['Article 371D','Continues in both states']]],
places:[['Hyderabad',78.47,17.38],['Amaravati',80.52,16.51],['Polavaram',81.65,17.25],['Visakhapatnam',83.3,17.7]],
figs:[],
qa:[['What were the main provisions of the Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act, 2014?',150,'The Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act, 2014 divided the state into Telangana and residual Andhra Pradesh with effect from 2 June 2014. Hyderabad was made the common capital of both states for a period not exceeding ten years. Assets, liabilities, employees and institutions were divided between the two states, with mechanisms to resolve disputes. The Polavaram irrigation project was declared a national project, to be executed by the centre. The Act promised the residual state new national institutions, such as IIT, IIM and AIIMS, and support for building a new capital and infrastructure. River water management boards were created for the Krishna and Godavari. Article 371D continued to apply in both states, and the Act provided for tax incentives and development of backward districts. Several issues, such as division of institutions and funds, took years to settle, and some remain under discussion between the two states.'],
['Discuss the challenges faced by residual Andhra Pradesh after bifurcation.',250,'When Telangana was formed on 2 June 2014, residual Andhra Pradesh, consisting of Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema, faced serious challenges.\n\nThe first was the loss of Hyderabad, the capital and economic centre built over decades. Most major industries, IT companies, central institutions and higher education centres were in Hyderabad, which also generated a large share of state revenue. Residual AP had to build a new capital from scratch; it chose Amaravati, but the project faced funding, land and policy problems.\n\nSecond, the state faced a revenue deficit. It inherited a larger share of population and debt but a smaller share of revenue, creating fiscal stress. The promised special category status, which would have given greater central assistance, became a major political issue when it was not granted, though a special assistance package was offered.\n\nThird, infrastructure and industry had to be developed. Projects such as Polavaram, declared a national project to irrigate large areas and supply water, faced delays and cost escalation. Ports like Visakhapatnam and Krishnapatnam, industrial corridors and new institutions were seen as engines of growth.\n\nFourth, regional balance within the state remained a concern, especially the drought-prone Rayalaseema and backward north coastal districts.\n\nFifth, sharing of river waters, assets, employees and institutions with Telangana led to disputes that took years to settle.\n\nAt the same time, the state has opportunities: a long coastline, ports, agriculture in fertile deltas, and a young workforce. Addressing capital development, fiscal health, irrigation and industrialisation remains central to its future.']]});

X('ap-culture',{lesson:'NIOS Lesson 8 (Telugu Mahabharata completed by Tikkana), Lesson 14 (Swaramela Kalanidhi of Ramamatya, Kondavidu) and AP sources',
brief:'NIOS notes, in its early medieval lesson, that regional languages grew and that the Telugu Mahabharata was completed by poet Tikkana in the 13th century, and in its medieval culture lesson, the Swaramela Kalanidhi (1550) of Ramamatya of Kondavidu on ragas. Telugu literature began with Nannaya (11th century); the Kavitrayam (Nannaya, Tikkana, Errana) completed the Mahabharata; Potana wrote the Bhagavatam; Srinatha flourished under the Reddis; the Vijayanagara age produced prabandha poetry under the Ashtadiggajas; modern writers include Gurajada, Viswanatha Satyanarayana (first Telugu Jnanpith) and Sri Sri. Culture also includes Kuchipudi, Burrakatha, Tholu Bommalata and Kalamkari.',
revise:['Eastern Chalukya patronage → Nannaya begins the Telugu Mahabharata (11th century).','Kakatiya and Reddi patronage → Tikkana and Errana complete it → Kavitrayam.','Bhakti → Potana’s Bhagavatam, Annamacharya’s songs.','Vijayanagara court → prabandha (Ashtadiggajas, Peddana) → golden age.','Colonial modernity → prose, reform literature (Veeresalingam, Gurajada) → progressive poetry (Sri Sri).','Performing arts (Kuchipudi, Burrakatha) + crafts (Kalamkari) → living culture.'],
analysis:['Telugu literature grew through translation (Kavitrayam), devotion (Potana) and courtly poetry (prabandha), showing different patrons and audiences.','Modern Telugu literature turned to social realism and reform, reflecting colonial and national change.','Culture is an important part of APPSC Mains; linking literature to dynasties gives answers depth.'],
mind:['Telugu literature and culture',[['Early',['Nannaya','Tikkana','Errana']],['Bhakti',['Potana','Annamacharya','Vemana']],['Prabandha',['Srinatha','Peddana','Ashtadiggajas']],['Modern',['Veeresalingam','Gurajada','Viswanatha','Sri Sri']],['Arts',['Kuchipudi','Burrakatha','Tholu Bommalata','Kalamkari']]]],
table:['Telugu literature through the ages',['Age','Poet','Work','Patron'],[['11th century','Nannaya','Andhra Mahabharatam (begun)','Rajaraja Narendra (E. Chalukya)'],['13th century','Tikkana','Mahabharata (15 parvas)','Nellore Cholas / Kakatiya era'],['14th century','Errana','Completed Aranya parva','Reddis'],['15th century','Potana','Bhagavatam','—'],['15th century','Srinatha','Sringara Naishadham','Pedakomati Vema Reddi'],['16th century','Allasani Peddana','Manucharitra','Krishnadevaraya'],['20th century','Viswanatha Satyanarayana','Ramayana Kalpavrukshamu','Jnanpith 1970']]],
places:[['Rajahmundry (Nannaya)',81.78,17.0],['Nellore (Tikkana)',79.98,14.44],['Bammera (Potana)',79.43,17.9],['Kondaveedu (Srinatha)',80.25,16.25],['Hampi (Ashtadiggajas)',76.46,15.33],['Tallapaka (Annamacharya)',79.0,14.17],['Kuchipudi',80.98,16.24]],
figs:[],
qa:[['Write a note on the Kavitrayam.',150,'The Kavitrayam, or “trinity of poets”, refers to Nannaya, Tikkana and Errana, who together translated the Sanskrit Mahabharata into Telugu, creating the Andhra Mahabharatam. Nannaya, the court poet of the Eastern Chalukya king Rajaraja Narendra in the 11th century, began the work, completing the Adi and Sabha parvas and part of the Aranya parva; he also shaped Telugu grammar and style and is called the Adikavi. Tikkana, in the 13th century, translated fifteen parvas, from the Virata parva to the end, with dramatic power; NIOS notes that he completed the Telugu Mahabharata. Errana, in the 14th century under the Reddis, completed the unfinished part of the Aranya parva. Their work established Telugu as a major literary language. The Kavitrayam’s Mahabharata remains one of the greatest works of Telugu literature, read and recited to this day.'],
['Trace the development of Telugu literature from Nannaya to the modern age.',250,'Telugu literature has developed over nearly a thousand years, reflecting changing patrons, beliefs and society.\n\nIt began in the 11th century with Nannaya, court poet of the Eastern Chalukya king Rajaraja Narendra, who started translating the Mahabharata. Tikkana in the 13th century continued it, and Errana in the 14th century completed it; NIOS notes Tikkana’s completion of the Telugu Mahabharata. This age of translation gave Telugu its literary form.\n\nNext came devotional literature. Bammera Potana’s Andhra Mahabhagavatam (15th century) is loved for its devotion and beauty. Annamacharya composed thousands of songs to Lord Venkateswara, and Vemana’s simple verses criticised social evils.\n\nThe Reddi court produced Srinatha, Kavisarvabhauma, whose works and travels reflect society. The Vijayanagara age, especially under Krishnadevaraya, was the golden age of prabandha, elaborate narrative poems. The Ashtadiggajas, including Allasani Peddana (Manucharitra) and Tenali Ramakrishna, flourished, and Krishnadevaraya himself wrote Amuktamalyada. The Nayaka courts of Thanjavur and Madurai continued Telugu literature in the south.\n\nThe modern age began in the 19th century with prose and social reform. Kandukuri Veeresalingam wrote novels and essays; Gurajada Apparao’s Kanyasulkam used spoken Telugu to satirise society; Gidugu Ramamurthy championed spoken language. In the 20th century, the romantic Bhavakavitvam movement (Devulapalli Krishnasastri), Viswanatha Satyanarayana’s Ramayana Kalpavrukshamu (first Telugu Jnanpith, 1970) and Sri Sri’s revolutionary Maha Prasthanam showed new directions, followed by progressive, Dalit and feminist writing.\n\nThus Telugu literature moved from translation and devotion to courtly elegance and finally to social realism, mirroring the history of the Telugu people.']]});
